<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vincent Lauinger<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">(1),<math id="m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math></sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lennart Schmitz<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">(2)</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Patrick Matalla<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">(2)</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Andrej Rode<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup">(1)</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sebastian Randel<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">(2)</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
<br class="ltx_break">Laurent Schmalen<sup id="id6" class="ltx_sup">(1)</sup>
</span></span></div>

<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SDM</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SDM</abbr>) allows to overcome the information capacity limitations of <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SSMF</span> by exploiting multiple spatial channels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and, potentially, decrease the energy consumption per bit by sharing hardware. While randomly-coupled <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MCF</span> are beneficial, e.g., in their tolerance to nonlinearities, they require more challenging  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MIMO</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MIMO</abbr>)  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">DSP</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">DSP</abbr>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. By avoiding the overhead from pilot transmission, blind algorithms can increase the net data rate or the robustness of the system by allowing a higher  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FEC</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FEC</abbr>) overhead. The state-of-the-art blind  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CMA</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CMA</abbr>) is sub-optimal for higher-order modulation formats and suffers from convergence issues for  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PCS</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PCS</abbr>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, which became integral to optical communication systems due to its benefits, e.g., simple rate adaption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. A promising alternative is the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> which got attention in the past years <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and also enables  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">JCAS</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">JCAS</abbr>) by combining  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ML</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ML</abbr>)-approximating channel estimation with excellent equalization performance. However, to the best of our knowledge, this approach has not been analyzed with data from optical transmission experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> leverages statistical inference techniques to approximate the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ML</abbr> channel estimate and, as a byproduct, equalizes the received signal. The concept boils down to a loss function <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, often denoted as the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ELBO</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ELBO</abbr>), which only depends on the statistics of the transmitted signal, i.e., the prior distribution, when using, e.g., a butterfly structure with  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FIR</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FIR</abbr>) filters as channel estimator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Hence, this approach is blind in the sense that it does not require pilot tones or a known transmit sequence.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.14072v1/VAE_scheme_both.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:20.783em; height:10.341em;aspect-ratio:276/137;" width="276" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Block diagram of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> with equalizer block (Eq.), channel estimator (Est.) and soft demapper (Soft Demap.). The <span id="S2.F1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#A12121;">red</span> elements, i.e., the pale box including the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> block and the phase rotation in the loss function, are only applicable for the proposed <span id="S2.F1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PC-VAEE</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The basic scheme of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> is depicted in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization ‣ Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The noisy received signal <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is fed into an arbitrary equalizer function, which is parameterized by weights <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and followed by a soft demapper to obtain estimates <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{q}</annotation></semantics></math> of the posterior distribution. These soft values can be used for soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FEC</abbr> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and are input to the loss function <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, which consists of two terms: <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the relative entropy (also known as Kullback-Leibler divergence) between the real prior and the approximated posterior <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, which favors output distributions similar to the prior; and <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the expectation <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\bm{q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝒒</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\bm{q}}</annotation></semantics></math> (with respect to <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{q}</annotation></semantics></math>) of the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MSE</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MSE</abbr>) between the noisy received signal <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> and the projected received signal, which can be computed from the estimated channel taps <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the estimated posterior <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and the discrete modulation alphabet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. An arbitrary optimizer, e.g., Adam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> or simple gradient descent, can be used to minimize the loss by jointly updating the equalizer and channel estimator weights.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.14072v1/experimental_setup_orig.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:45.638em; height:14.392em;aspect-ratio:606/191;" width="606" height="191"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Block diagram of the experimental SDM setup employing a randomly-coupled 4-core fiber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.14072v1/BMI_real_data.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:20.652em; height:12.405em;aspect-ratio:274/165;" width="274" height="165"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.14072v1/SNR_real_subfig.svg" id="S2.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:21.236em; height:11.802em;aspect-ratio:282/157;" width="282" height="157"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Results for the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SDM</abbr> transmission experiment with the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RC-4CF</abbr>: bit-wise mutual information (BMI) in (a) and estimated signal-to-noise ratio in (b). The markers denote the <math id="S2.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mean" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="1.110em">mean</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mean</annotation></semantics></math>, the shaded area is limited by the corresponding <math id="S2.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math> values.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An important aspect of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> is its inherent phase-sensitive nature, which is a major difference to the popular  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CM</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CM</abbr>) loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
As a consequence, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> is able to track and compensate phase fluctuations to some degree; however, strong phase noise requires perpetual adaptation and, eventually, may prevent proper convergence of the equalizer. In the latter case, a trailing <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> algorithm can be introduced after the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> by leaving the structure as introduced before (where <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to compute the loss), while compensating the remaining phase error of the equalized signal <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> and getting a phase-corrected estimate <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\bm{q}}\neq\bm{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒒</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mi>𝒒</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\bm{q}}\neq\bm{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for further evaluation. Similarly to <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CM</abbr>-based equalizers, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> is not part of the update procedure and, thus, does not have to be differentiable. However, potential convergence issues due to perpetual adaptation remain. In the following, this variant of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> is denoted <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE+CPR</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a non-trivial approach to include <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> to the update procedure as indicated by the red elements in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization ‣ Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, where two points have to be considered: first, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> has to be differentiable, which is, e.g., not the case for the plain  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">BPS</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">BPS</abbr>) algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. However, we can substitute the non-differentiable <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{argmax}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>argmax</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{argmax}</annotation></semantics></math> operation with a <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{softmax+temperature}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>temperature</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{softmax+temperature}</annotation></semantics></math> during gradient propagation as used for end-to-end learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Second, the loss function has to be adapted since the compensated phase error in the equalized signal has to be learned by the channel estimator as well <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. As indicated by the red term in the loss function, this problem can be solved by rotating the projected transmit symbols with the negative phase offset estimate <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the following, this variant of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> is denoted <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PC-VAEE</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As a reference, we update the equalizer, consisting of the same butterfly structure with <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FIR</abbr> filters as the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> variants, with an Adam optimizer using the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CM</abbr>-loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. To implement the whole setup, we use <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MOKka</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, an open-source library for machine learning in communications, and <span id="S2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Weights&amp;Biases</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> to find the best-fitting hyperparameters for each algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experimental Setup and Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We validate the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> in an <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SDM</abbr> transmission experiment, where eight <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\text{\,}\mathrm{G}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>GBd</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\text{\,}\mathrm{G}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{d}</annotation></semantics></math> 16-QAM signals are transmitted through <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150\text{\,}\mathrm{k}\mathrm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>150</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>km</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150\text{\,}\mathrm{k}\mathrm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RC-4CF</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RC-4CF</abbr>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, as thoroughly described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and depicted in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization ‣ Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ECL</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ECL</abbr>) has a nominal linewidth of <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\text{\,}\mathrm{k}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>kHz</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\text{\,}\mathrm{k}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and the fiber’s overall group delay spread caused by  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MD</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MD</abbr>) is specified to <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\text{\,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mtext> </mtext><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\text{\,}</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\text{\,}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{/}\sqrt{km}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>ps</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\text{\,}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{s}\mathrm{/}\sqrt{km}</annotation></semantics></math>. At the receiver, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">DSP</abbr> chain includes resampling to two  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sps</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sps</abbr>), carrier-frequency offset compensation based on a single polarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>,  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CD</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CD</abbr>) compensation using the overlap-and-save algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, and a joint clock recovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The equalizer consists of a complex-valued <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\!\times\!8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\!\times\!8</annotation></semantics></math> butterfly structure of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FIR</abbr> filters with <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathrm{eq}}\!=\!151" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>151</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathrm{eq}}\!=\!151</annotation></semantics></math> taps, which are updated batch-wise over <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{batch}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>batch</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{batch}}</annotation></semantics></math> symbols. The batches overlap such that no extra padding is required for the filters. The channel estimators of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> are constructed similarly to the equalizers. If <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> is applicable, we use a differentiable <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">BPS</abbr> algorithm with temperature <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!=\!0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!=\!0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, 40 test angles per quadrant, and triangular averaging window of length <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{cpr}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>cpr</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{cpr}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
After each frame of <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\,000\text{\,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10 000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\,000\text{\,}</annotation></semantics></math> symbols, we estimate the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">BMI</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">BMI</abbr>) per channel. For each run, 100 frames are processed and the last 20 estimates are stored. Thus, each depicted value is the <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\min,\ \max,\ \mathrm{mean}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>min</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>max</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>mean</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\min,\ \max,\ \mathrm{mean}\}</annotation></semantics></math> over 200 estimates from 10 independent runs of the same configuration.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.14072v1/BER_woPCS.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:20.652em; height:12.405em;aspect-ratio:274/165;" width="274" height="165"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.14072v1/SNR_woPCS.svg" id="S3.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:21.236em; height:11.802em;aspect-ratio:282/157;" width="282" height="157"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Results for the simulation with uniform 64-QAM at <math id="S3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\text{\,}\mathrm{G}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>GBd</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\text{\,}\mathrm{G}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and very high linewidth <math id="S3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{f}=$1\text{\,}\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{z}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>MHz</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{f}=$1\text{\,}\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{z}$</annotation></semantics></math>: bit-wise mutual information (BMI) in (a) and estimated signal-to-noise ratio in (b). The markers denote the <math id="S3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mean" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="1.110em">mean</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mean</annotation></semantics></math>, the shaded area is limited by the corresponding <math id="S3.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math> values.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A pilot-aided <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MSE</abbr> loss with integrated supervised phase recovery is used for pre-convergence over one frame, i.e., <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\,000\text{\,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10 000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\,000\text{\,}</annotation></semantics></math> symbols. Note that the resulting <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BMI}\!&lt;\!0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>BMI</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BMI}\!&lt;\!0.05</annotation></semantics></math> ( <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SER</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SER</abbr>)<math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\!0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\!0.9</annotation></semantics></math>) is not sufficient to switch to a decision-directed <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MSE</abbr> loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization ‣ Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a), the reference blind <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">CM-loss</span> as well as the proposed <span id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PC-VAEE</span> converge properly while the <span id="S3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE+CPR</span> and the plain <span id="S3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE</span> suffer from penalties. For the latter two, the <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math> of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">BMI</abbr> is significantly higher than the <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mean}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>mean</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mean}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that the filters converged but the rapidly varying phase offset consistently prevents proper demapping. The difference in the shaded areas, i.e., the area between the corresponding <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>min</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">BMI</abbr>, of the <span id="S3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE</span> and the <span id="S3.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE+CPR</span> is only due to compensation of the remaining phase offset, since the update procedure is the same. Thus, only the proposed <span id="S3.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PC-VAEE</span> fully benefits from <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr>.
As the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr> acts as <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ML</abbr>-approximating channel estimators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, it enables <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">JCAS</abbr> by monitoring the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SNR</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SNR</abbr>) and impulse responses of the channel. Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization ‣ Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) depicts the estimated channel <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{est}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>est</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{est}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the processing of Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2 Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization ‣ Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a).
If the algorithms have not converged properly (e.g., the <span id="S3.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE</span> and the <span id="S3.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE+CPR</span>), the <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{est}}=P_{\mathrm{sig}}\cdot N/C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SNR</mi><mi>est</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>sig</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SNR}_{\mathrm{est}}=P_{\mathrm{sig}}\cdot N/C</annotation></semantics></math> is underestimated due to its inverse dependence on the <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>-term of <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Simulation Setup and Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To specifically analyze the influence of phase noise to the proposed <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr>, we simulate the transmission of uniform 64- <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">QAM</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">QAM</abbr>) over a simple model of an uncoupled 4-core <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MCF</abbr> at <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathrm{S}}\!=\!$90\text{\,}\mathrm{G}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{d}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mn>90</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>GBd</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathrm{S}}\!=\!$90\text{\,}\mathrm{G}\mathrm{B}\mathrm{d}$</annotation></semantics></math> and different laser linewidth <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. The transmitted pulses are shaped by a  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RRC</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RRC</abbr>) filter with roll-off of 0.2 and the received sequence is sampled with 2 <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">sps</abbr>. Each core is modeled as linear optical dual-polarization channel with the frequency domain channel matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{H}\left(f\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{H}\left(f\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bm{R}\left(\gamma_{\text{hv}}\right)\begin{pmatrix}{\mathrm{e}}^{{\mathrm{j}}\pi\tau_{\text{pmd}}f}&amp;0\\
0&{\mathrm{e}}^{-{\mathrm{j}}\pi\tau_{\text{pmd}}f}\end{pmatrix}{\mathrm{e}}^{-{\mathrm{j}}2\pi^{2}\beta_{\text{cd}}L_{\text{cd}}f^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑹</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>hv</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>pmd</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>pmd</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>j2</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>cd</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>cd</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bm{R}\left(\gamma_{\text{hv}}\right)\begin{pmatrix}{\mathrm{e}}^{{\mathrm{j}}\pi\tau_{\text{pmd}}f}&amp;0\\
0&amp;{\mathrm{e}}^{-{\mathrm{j}}\pi\tau_{\text{pmd}}f}\end{pmatrix}{\mathrm{e}}^{-{\mathrm{j}}2\pi^{2}\beta_{\text{cd}}L_{\text{cd}}f^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with rotation matrix <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{R}</annotation></semantics></math> describing a rotation by <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\text{hv}}\!=\!\frac{\pi}{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mtext>hv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\text{hv}}\!=\!\frac{\pi}{10}</annotation></semantics></math>, differential group delay <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{pmd}}\!=\!\sqrt{$1000\text{\,}$}\cdot$0.1\text{\,}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{s}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>pmd</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1000</mn><mtext> </mtext><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>ps</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{pmd}}\!=\!\sqrt{$1000\text{\,}$}\cdot$0.1\text{\,}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{s}$</annotation></semantics></math> causing first-order  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PMD</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PMD</abbr>), and residual <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CD</abbr> of <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\beta_{\text{cd}}L_{\mathrm{cd}}\!=\!-$26\text{\,}\mathrm{ps}$\cdot$2\text{\,}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>cd</mtext></msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>cd</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>26</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">ps</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\text{cd}}L_{\mathrm{cd}}\!=\!-$26\text{\,}\mathrm{ps}$\cdot$2\text{\,}$</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. The resulting eight signals are randomly permuted and distorted by different phase noise realizations of a Wiener process with variance <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\Delta\varphi}\!=\!2\pi\delta_{f}/R_{\mathrm{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\Delta\varphi}\!=\!2\pi\delta_{f}/R_{\mathrm{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">AWGN</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">AWGN</abbr>) such that the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SNR</abbr> is <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\text{\,}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>dB</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\text{\,}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. We apply the same averaging scheme to the estimates and construct the equalizer, channel estimator and <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> similarly to the section above with <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\mathrm{eq}}\!=\!M_{\mathrm{est}}\!=\!51" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>est</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>51</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\mathrm{eq}}\!=\!M_{\mathrm{est}}\!=\!51</annotation></semantics></math> taps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results are depicted in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3 Experimental Setup and Results ‣ Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Similarly to the experimental results, the <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PC-VAEE</span> converges reliably for uniform 64-QAM even with very high linewith (<math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{f}\!=\!$1\text{\,}\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{z}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>MHz</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{f}\!=\!$1\text{\,}\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{z}$</annotation></semantics></math>), as depicted in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3 Experimental Setup and Results ‣ Novel Phase-Noise-Tolerant Variational-Autoencoder-Based Equalization Suitable for Space-Division-Multiplexed Transmission" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a) and (b), and estimates the simulated <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SNR</abbr> of <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\text{\,}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>dB</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\text{\,}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{B}</annotation></semantics></math> very accurately. Interestingly, the <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE</span> and <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE+CPR</span>, which have the same update procedure, only show relatively small penalties in their <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>max</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max</annotation></semantics></math> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">BMI</abbr> for the fast-varying phase distortions.
While the trailing <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> compensates remaining phase errors for the <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE+CPR</span>  the performance of the <span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">VAEE</span> is degraded significantly.
The relatively worse <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SNR</abbr> estimation reveals that, in this scenario, the algorithms rely more on the <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-term of <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> (KL-divergence) than the <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>-term, which is relevant for <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SNR</abbr> estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrated the potential of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VAEE</abbr>, in particular, the proposed <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PC-VAEE</span>, in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SDM</abbr> setups, especially in combination with <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">JCAS</abbr>. A good <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CPR</abbr> is crucial for application in fiber-optic communication systems, where phase noise can be significant. Future work may focus on systems which employ <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">PCS</abbr>, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">JCAS</abbr> aspect, or the further applications.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Acknowledgements</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work has received funding from the German Federal Ministry of Education and Research (BMBF) under grant agreement 16KIS1420 (STARFALL) and the European Research Council (ERC) under the EU’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme (grant agreement 101001899).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\defbibnote</span>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">myprenoteCitations must be easy and quick to find. More precisely:</p>
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Please list all the authors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The title must be given in full length.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Journal and conference names should not be abbreviated but rather given in full length.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The DOI number should be added incl. a link.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
